<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01524</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e54236c4-913e-4fc3-9e4c-4578e808decb</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson. Double click on mathematical
  expressions/equations to enlarge. If needed, use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
      <!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <exercise id="54828607-798d-48f4-9098-8816f83d3f26" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Whenever the input of a function \(f\) increases by 1, the output increases by 5. Which of these equations
          could define \(f\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00003">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00004">\(f(x)=3x+5\)</para>
            <para id="para-00005">\(f(x)=5x+3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00006">\( f(x)=5^x\)</para>
            <para id="para-00007">\( f(x)=x^5\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00008">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2. 
      </para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is \(f(x)=5x+3\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="4c75059f-e45f-4203-9d6d-2787a3c8b931" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00011">The function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=2^x\). Which of the following statements is true about the values of
          \(f\)? <emphasis>Select all </emphasis>that apply. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is b and d. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="968c16ab-3a12-494b-9d43-4b32752c1cab" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">The two lines on the coordinate plane are graphs of functions \(f\) and \(g\). Use for the graph below for
        questions 3 and 4.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">
              <figure id="fig-00001">
                <media alt="Graph of 2 lines.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_17_10_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00017">Use the graph to explain why the value of \(f\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by
          1. 
        </para>
            <para id="para-00018">Multiple Choice:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2. 
      </para>
            <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is: \((0,0)\) and \((1,2)\) are two points on the graph of \(f\). Between the two points,
        \(y\) increases by 2 and \(x\) increases by 1.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a146c478-5f35-4970-b07d-8d9594463256" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00022">Use the graph to explain why the value of \(g\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by 1.
          <emphasis>Select two </emphasis>that apply.
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is a and d. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="1107dbf8-66fc-45a5-9745-97e5975d3473" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00026">The function \(h\) is given by \(h(x)=5^x\). Find the quotient \(\frac{h(x+2)}{h(x)}\). 
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00027">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.3. 
      </para>
            <para id="para-00029">The correct answer is 25. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="5674259f-b084-4426-bbff-5cf458fbcbd1" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00030">The function \(h\) is given by \(h(x)=5^x\). Find the quotient \(\frac{h(x+3)}{h(x)}\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00031">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">The correct answer is 125. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="68556bd3-af50-4ef9-b72b-3b62d0a83449" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00034">For each of the functions \(f,g,h,p\), and \(q\), the domain is \(0≤x≤100\). For which functions is
          the average rate of change a good measure of how the function changes for this domain? <emphasis>Select three</emphasis> that
          apply. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00035">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00036">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.4. 
      </para>
            <para id="para-00037">The correct answer is \(f(x)=x+2, h(x)=111x−23, q(x)=87.5\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="2ba83d50-cf33-400c-a317-9a186fa46fc3" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00038">What would be the rate of change of the following table?</para>
            <table class="os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00001">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                \(x\)
              </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                \(f(x)\)
              </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                1
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                -4
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                -2
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                3
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                0
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                5
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00039">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00040">Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.2.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00041">The correct answer is 2.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="9c739c4f-ee63-4f46-bfdd-8ccfeb05fbc7" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00042">Given the following table, what would be \(\frac {f(x+2)}{f(x+1)}\)?</para>
            <table class="os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00002">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                \(x\)
              </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                \(f(x)\)
              </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                1
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                3
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                8
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                16
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                5
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                32
              </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00043">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00044">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.3. 
      </para>
            <para id="para-00045">The correct answer is 2.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bf41e467-06b4-41c1-8496-accddf0ee0a0" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00046">Which of the following statements compare rates of \(f(x) = 3x +2\) and \(g(x)=3^x\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00047">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00048">In \(f\) and \(g\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00049">In \(f\) and \(g\) you add 3 to get to the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00050">In \(f\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \(g\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00051">In \(g\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \(f\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00052">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00053">Try Again!</para>
            <para id="para-00054">You seem to be struggling. Who can you ask for help?</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
